Because $\Delta k=1/r$, define continuum-normalized states at the sampled wave numbers by $\ket{k_n}:=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Delta k}}\,
    \ket{k_n}_{\mathrm{box}}
    =\sqrt r\,\ket{k_n}_{\mathrm{box}}.$ Their position-space kernels are $\braket{x}{k_n}
    =\sqrt r\,\frac{e^{ik_nx}}{\sqrt{2\pi r}}
    =\frac{e^{ik_nx}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}.$ The factor of $\sqrt r$ has not vanished. It has converted a unit-normalized discrete state into a delta-normalized continuum state. Indeed, $\braket{k_m}{k_n}
    =\frac{\delta_{mn}}{\Delta k},$ which tends distributionally to $\braket{k'}{k}=\delta(k-k').$ The peak grows like $1/\Delta k=r$ while its width shrinks like $\Delta k$, leaving unit area.

