Insert the position resolution of identity into $\braket k\psi$: $\begin{aligned}
  \widetilde\psi(k)
    &=\int dx\,\braket kx\braket x\psi\\
    &=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
      \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\,e^{-ikx}\psi(x).
  \end{aligned}$ Insert the momentum resolution into $\braket x\psi$: $\begin{aligned}
  \psi(x)
    &=\int dk\,\braket xk\braket k\psi\\
    &=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
      \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dk\,e^{ikx}\widetilde\psi(k).
  \end{aligned}$ These are the forward and inverse Fourier transforms. Fourier analysis is older than quantum mechanics; quantum mechanics interprets the transform as a change between position and momentum descriptions.

