p.src:
For an arbitrary linear-polarization angle $\theta$, the announced generalization is $\begin{aligned}
  \ket{\theta}
  &=\cos\theta\,\ket x+\sin\theta\,\ket y,\\
  \ket{\theta_\perp}
  &=-\sin\theta\,\ket x+\cos\theta\,\ket y.
  \end{aligned}$ The corresponding observable has these two states as eigenvectors. Rotating the axis by $90^\circ$ merely exchanges the outcomes and reverses the sign of the same observable. Distinct nonorthogonal axes give genuinely incompatible tests. Complex relative coefficients introduce circular and elliptical polarization; that extension is reserved for the next lecture.

